const e=JSON.parse(`[{"id":"RC_E3_001","exercise":"exercise_3","passage_id":"RC_E3_P1","passage":"In his book An Account of the Cultivation and Manufacture of Tea in China (1848), Samuel Ball wrote that Chinese tea merchants used the burning incense sticks to track the time of various stages of tea roasting. 'The time of roasting,' he wrote, was 'regulated by means of the instrument denominated a Che Hiang' (or zhi xiang, meaning 'stick of incense'). In this, the Chinese industry shared much in common with the massive, nascent industrial sugar plantations of the Caribbean, which had matured around the same period. According to the American anthropologist Sidney Mintz, writing in his book Sweetness and Power (1985), two factors explain the industrial sense of time-discipline that characterized these large estates. First, there was the natural pressure to process sugarcane before it spoiled, often within a day; and, second, planters felt the social pressure of market competition to minimise production costs. Similar dynamics could be observed on the other side of the world, in the valleys of central China.\\n\\nBecause tea leaves are a natural, perishable product of the soil, their quality depends upon roasting, sifting and rolling in a timely fashion. At the time of Ball's conversations, merchants monitored these tasks out of concern for preserving the natural physical qualities of the final product. The units of time served as guidelines, but individual workers could adjust the duration as needed. 'Though the roastings are regulated by the measure of time designated a che hiang,' Ball wrote, 'yet that instrument is used more as a guide than a rule.' The workers were provided with samples and given the discretion to roast for longer or shorter periods, until the leaves acquired the right colour and appearance, as in a cooking recipe.\\n\\nWe also know that, decades after Ball's account, incense timekeepers began to be used to discipline labour practices in the southern districts of Anhui. Jiang Yaohua, a merchant from She county, Huizhou, operated a booming tea refinery in the market town of Tunxi, where he would ship thousands of pounds of leaves to Shanghai each spring. In his handbook for tea production, he stated that the roasters would first stir the leaves in cool air until '80 per cent to a full stick of incense has been used' and then press the leaves down into the heat for 'half a stick of incense'. Finally, the worker was to leave the pot directly over the fire for the duration of burning two and three-quarter incense sticks.\\n\\nThe process outlined in Jiang handbook followed Ball's earlier account insofar as it concerned itself with maintaining a high-quality standard for the product. But now it was also designed to create a full itinerary for an 18-incense-stick – or 12-hour – workday. Jiang Yaohua mapped out a schedule that maximised the amount of productive activity possible given the physical limits of his human workforce. Decades later, a social surveyor named Fan Hejun would visit the Huizhou tea districts and comment on the effects of this 18-stick workday. 'During that time,' he wrote, 'one day of roasting labour completely depletes all muscle strength.' Fan Hejun further observed that the incense sticks now burned to regulate the wages of hired workers: Taking four baskets as one shift, the skilled workers can earn four units of wages, with each unit worth [about 15 cents]. The unskilled workers who can only roast two baskets per shift only earn, in the span of 18 incense sticks, two units of wages. Within this action-packed schedule, the incense sticks were used to keep workers on task, leaving little room for the flexible attitude towards completing tasks seen in Ball's day.","question":"In An Account of the Cultivation and Manufacture of Tea in China, Samuel Ball wrote that incense sticks were used in tea production to","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"keep time by tracking the burning of incense.","B":"burn slowly at a regular rate for timekeeping.","C":"keep track of time in the tea-making process.","D":"preserve tradition by means of an exotic timekeeping device."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"keep track of time in the tea-making process.","difficulty":"easy","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"correct_but_unstated","B":"correct_but_unstated","D":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"Choice A refers to process, not purpose. Choice B also refers to process, not purpose. Choice C is CORRECT — refer to 'Samuel Ball wrote that Chinese tea merchants used the burning incense sticks to track the time of various stages of tea roasting.' The purpose was to keep track of time in the tea-making process. Choice D is not explicitly cited in the passage.","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"RC_E3_002","exercise":"exercise_3","passage_id":"RC_E3_P1","passage":"In his book An Account of the Cultivation and Manufacture of Tea in China (1848), Samuel Ball wrote that Chinese tea merchants used the burning incense sticks to track the time of various stages of tea roasting. 'The time of roasting,' he wrote, was 'regulated by means of the instrument denominated a Che Hiang' (or zhi xiang, meaning 'stick of incense'). In this, the Chinese industry shared much in common with the massive, nascent industrial sugar plantations of the Caribbean, which had matured around the same period. According to the American anthropologist Sidney Mintz, writing in his book Sweetness and Power (1985), two factors explain the industrial sense of time-discipline that characterized these large estates. First, there was the natural pressure to process sugarcane before it spoiled, often within a day; and, second, planters felt the social pressure of market competition to minimise production costs. Similar dynamics could be observed on the other side of the world, in the valleys of central China.\\n\\nBecause tea leaves are a natural, perishable product of the soil, their quality depends upon roasting, sifting and rolling in a timely fashion. At the time of Ball's conversations, merchants monitored these tasks out of concern for preserving the natural physical qualities of the final product. The units of time served as guidelines, but individual workers could adjust the duration as needed. 'Though the roastings are regulated by the measure of time designated a che hiang,' Ball wrote, 'yet that instrument is used more as a guide than a rule.' The workers were provided with samples and given the discretion to roast for longer or shorter periods, until the leaves acquired the right colour and appearance, as in a cooking recipe.\\n\\nWe also know that, decades after Ball's account, incense timekeepers began to be used to discipline labour practices in the southern districts of Anhui. Jiang Yaohua, a merchant from She county, Huizhou, operated a booming tea refinery in the market town of Tunxi, where he would ship thousands of pounds of leaves to Shanghai each spring. In his handbook for tea production, he stated that the roasters would first stir the leaves in cool air until '80 per cent to a full stick of incense has been used' and then press the leaves down into the heat for 'half a stick of incense'. Finally, the worker was to leave the pot directly over the fire for the duration of burning two and three-quarter incense sticks.\\n\\nThe process outlined in Jiang handbook followed Ball's earlier account insofar as it concerned itself with maintaining a high-quality standard for the product. But now it was also designed to create a full itinerary for an 18-incense-stick – or 12-hour – workday. Jiang Yaohua mapped out a schedule that maximised the amount of productive activity possible given the physical limits of his human workforce. Decades later, a social surveyor named Fan Hejun would visit the Huizhou tea districts and comment on the effects of this 18-stick workday. 'During that time,' he wrote, 'one day of roasting labour completely depletes all muscle strength.' Fan Hejun further observed that the incense sticks now burned to regulate the wages of hired workers: Taking four baskets as one shift, the skilled workers can earn four units of wages, with each unit worth [about 15 cents]. The unskilled workers who can only roast two baskets per shift only earn, in the span of 18 incense sticks, two units of wages. Within this action-packed schedule, the incense sticks were used to keep workers on task, leaving little room for the flexible attitude towards completing tasks seen in Ball's day.","question":"The author cites Sweetness and Power to make the case that","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"market pressures forced tea producers to increase productivity in an industrial manner.","B":"rural tea production in China exhibited social dynamics that belong within the modern history of capitalism.","C":"incense sticks were employed in the tea industry in response to a rising global demand followed by plummeting prices.","D":"tea merchants remained profitable by asking laborers to work harder, faster, and for less reward."},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"market pressures forced tea producers to increase productivity in an industrial manner.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"B":"out_of_scope","C":"out_of_scope","D":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"Choice A is CORRECT — refer to 'planters felt the social pressure of market competition to minimise production costs.' The citation of Sweetness and Power supports the case that market pressures drove industrial time-discipline. Choice B is incorrect because what the social dynamics are is not specifically mentioned in the cited book. Choice C is not specifically stated in the passage. Choice D is not specifically stated in the passage.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E3_003","exercise":"exercise_3","passage_id":"RC_E3_P1","passage":"In his book An Account of the Cultivation and Manufacture of Tea in China (1848), Samuel Ball wrote that Chinese tea merchants used the burning incense sticks to track the time of various stages of tea roasting. 'The time of roasting,' he wrote, was 'regulated by means of the instrument denominated a Che Hiang' (or zhi xiang, meaning 'stick of incense'). In this, the Chinese industry shared much in common with the massive, nascent industrial sugar plantations of the Caribbean, which had matured around the same period. According to the American anthropologist Sidney Mintz, writing in his book Sweetness and Power (1985), two factors explain the industrial sense of time-discipline that characterized these large estates. First, there was the natural pressure to process sugarcane before it spoiled, often within a day; and, second, planters felt the social pressure of market competition to minimise production costs. Similar dynamics could be observed on the other side of the world, in the valleys of central China.\\n\\nBecause tea leaves are a natural, perishable product of the soil, their quality depends upon roasting, sifting and rolling in a timely fashion. At the time of Ball's conversations, merchants monitored these tasks out of concern for preserving the natural physical qualities of the final product. The units of time served as guidelines, but individual workers could adjust the duration as needed. 'Though the roastings are regulated by the measure of time designated a che hiang,' Ball wrote, 'yet that instrument is used more as a guide than a rule.' The workers were provided with samples and given the discretion to roast for longer or shorter periods, until the leaves acquired the right colour and appearance, as in a cooking recipe.\\n\\nWe also know that, decades after Ball's account, incense timekeepers began to be used to discipline labour practices in the southern districts of Anhui. Jiang Yaohua, a merchant from She county, Huizhou, operated a booming tea refinery in the market town of Tunxi, where he would ship thousands of pounds of leaves to Shanghai each spring. In his handbook for tea production, he stated that the roasters would first stir the leaves in cool air until '80 per cent to a full stick of incense has been used' and then press the leaves down into the heat for 'half a stick of incense'. Finally, the worker was to leave the pot directly over the fire for the duration of burning two and three-quarter incense sticks.\\n\\nThe process outlined in Jiang handbook followed Ball's earlier account insofar as it concerned itself with maintaining a high-quality standard for the product. But now it was also designed to create a full itinerary for an 18-incense-stick – or 12-hour – workday. Jiang Yaohua mapped out a schedule that maximised the amount of productive activity possible given the physical limits of his human workforce. Decades later, a social surveyor named Fan Hejun would visit the Huizhou tea districts and comment on the effects of this 18-stick workday. 'During that time,' he wrote, 'one day of roasting labour completely depletes all muscle strength.' Fan Hejun further observed that the incense sticks now burned to regulate the wages of hired workers: Taking four baskets as one shift, the skilled workers can earn four units of wages, with each unit worth [about 15 cents]. The unskilled workers who can only roast two baskets per shift only earn, in the span of 18 incense sticks, two units of wages. Within this action-packed schedule, the incense sticks were used to keep workers on task, leaving little room for the flexible attitude towards completing tasks seen in Ball's day.","question":"According to Ball, time-consciousness in the tea producing system was dictated by","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"the use of coercion to exact labour.","B":"changing attitudes to the treatment of labour.","C":"the industrial cast to plantation enterprises.","D":"the nature of tea and its processing requirements."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"the nature of tea and its processing requirements.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"contradicts_passage","B":"out_of_scope","C":"contradicts_passage"},"explanation":"Choice A is not true according to the passage — Ball describes flexible, worker-adjusted timekeeping, not coercion. Choice B is not evident in the passage as such. Choice C is not true according to the passage — Ball's account precedes the industrial labour discipline that Jiang introduces. Choice D is CORRECT — refer to 'Because tea leaves are a natural, perishable product of the soil, their quality depends upon roasting, sifting and rolling in a timely fashion. At the time of Ball's conversations, merchants monitored these tasks out of concern for preserving the natural physical qualities of the final product.'","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E3_004","exercise":"exercise_3","passage_id":"RC_E3_P1","passage":"In his book An Account of the Cultivation and Manufacture of Tea in China (1848), Samuel Ball wrote that Chinese tea merchants used the burning incense sticks to track the time of various stages of tea roasting. 'The time of roasting,' he wrote, was 'regulated by means of the instrument denominated a Che Hiang' (or zhi xiang, meaning 'stick of incense'). In this, the Chinese industry shared much in common with the massive, nascent industrial sugar plantations of the Caribbean, which had matured around the same period. According to the American anthropologist Sidney Mintz, writing in his book Sweetness and Power (1985), two factors explain the industrial sense of time-discipline that characterized these large estates. First, there was the natural pressure to process sugarcane before it spoiled, often within a day; and, second, planters felt the social pressure of market competition to minimise production costs. Similar dynamics could be observed on the other side of the world, in the valleys of central China.\\n\\nBecause tea leaves are a natural, perishable product of the soil, their quality depends upon roasting, sifting and rolling in a timely fashion. At the time of Ball's conversations, merchants monitored these tasks out of concern for preserving the natural physical qualities of the final product. The units of time served as guidelines, but individual workers could adjust the duration as needed. 'Though the roastings are regulated by the measure of time designated a che hiang,' Ball wrote, 'yet that instrument is used more as a guide than a rule.' The workers were provided with samples and given the discretion to roast for longer or shorter periods, until the leaves acquired the right colour and appearance, as in a cooking recipe.\\n\\nWe also know that, decades after Ball's account, incense timekeepers began to be used to discipline labour practices in the southern districts of Anhui. Jiang Yaohua, a merchant from She county, Huizhou, operated a booming tea refinery in the market town of Tunxi, where he would ship thousands of pounds of leaves to Shanghai each spring. In his handbook for tea production, he stated that the roasters would first stir the leaves in cool air until '80 per cent to a full stick of incense has been used' and then press the leaves down into the heat for 'half a stick of incense'. Finally, the worker was to leave the pot directly over the fire for the duration of burning two and three-quarter incense sticks.\\n\\nThe process outlined in Jiang handbook followed Ball's earlier account insofar as it concerned itself with maintaining a high-quality standard for the product. But now it was also designed to create a full itinerary for an 18-incense-stick – or 12-hour – workday. Jiang Yaohua mapped out a schedule that maximised the amount of productive activity possible given the physical limits of his human workforce. Decades later, a social surveyor named Fan Hejun would visit the Huizhou tea districts and comment on the effects of this 18-stick workday. 'During that time,' he wrote, 'one day of roasting labour completely depletes all muscle strength.' Fan Hejun further observed that the incense sticks now burned to regulate the wages of hired workers: Taking four baskets as one shift, the skilled workers can earn four units of wages, with each unit worth [about 15 cents]. The unskilled workers who can only roast two baskets per shift only earn, in the span of 18 incense sticks, two units of wages. Within this action-packed schedule, the incense sticks were used to keep workers on task, leaving little room for the flexible attitude towards completing tasks seen in Ball's day.","question":"Which of the following statements can be inferred from Jiang Yaohua's handbook for tea production?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Changing attitudes towards the treatment of labour had an economic coefficient.","B":"No longer a passive function of tea refinement, time now actively regulated the workforce.","C":"Workers who did not own their own bodies, let alone their own labour, were united with the means of production.","D":"To detach the tea plantation from the emergent world economy that spawned them would be mistaken."},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"No longer a passive function of tea refinement, time now actively regulated the workforce.","difficulty":"hard","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"correct_but_unstated","C":"out_of_scope","D":"correct_but_unstated"},"explanation":"Choice A needs to be substantiated further and is not directly inferable from the handbook alone. Choice B is CORRECT — refer to 'The process outlined in Jiang handbook followed Ball's earlier account insofar as it concerned itself with maintaining a high-quality standard for the product. But now it was also designed to create a full itinerary for an 18-incense-stick – or 12-hour – workday.' Time shifted from being a product quality guide to an active regulator of worker behaviour. Choice C is not stated or implied in the passage. Choice D needs substantiation and goes beyond what the handbook alone supports.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E3_005","exercise":"exercise_3","passage_id":"RC_E3_P1","passage":"In his book An Account of the Cultivation and Manufacture of Tea in China (1848), Samuel Ball wrote that Chinese tea merchants used the burning incense sticks to track the time of various stages of tea roasting. 'The time of roasting,' he wrote, was 'regulated by means of the instrument denominated a Che Hiang' (or zhi xiang, meaning 'stick of incense'). In this, the Chinese industry shared much in common with the massive, nascent industrial sugar plantations of the Caribbean, which had matured around the same period. According to the American anthropologist Sidney Mintz, writing in his book Sweetness and Power (1985), two factors explain the industrial sense of time-discipline that characterized these large estates. First, there was the natural pressure to process sugarcane before it spoiled, often within a day; and, second, planters felt the social pressure of market competition to minimise production costs. Similar dynamics could be observed on the other side of the world, in the valleys of central China.\\n\\nBecause tea leaves are a natural, perishable product of the soil, their quality depends upon roasting, sifting and rolling in a timely fashion. At the time of Ball's conversations, merchants monitored these tasks out of concern for preserving the natural physical qualities of the final product. The units of time served as guidelines, but individual workers could adjust the duration as needed. 'Though the roastings are regulated by the measure of time designated a che hiang,' Ball wrote, 'yet that instrument is used more as a guide than a rule.' The workers were provided with samples and given the discretion to roast for longer or shorter periods, until the leaves acquired the right colour and appearance, as in a cooking recipe.\\n\\nWe also know that, decades after Ball's account, incense timekeepers began to be used to discipline labour practices in the southern districts of Anhui. Jiang Yaohua, a merchant from She county, Huizhou, operated a booming tea refinery in the market town of Tunxi, where he would ship thousands of pounds of leaves to Shanghai each spring. In his handbook for tea production, he stated that the roasters would first stir the leaves in cool air until '80 per cent to a full stick of incense has been used' and then press the leaves down into the heat for 'half a stick of incense'. Finally, the worker was to leave the pot directly over the fire for the duration of burning two and three-quarter incense sticks.\\n\\nThe process outlined in Jiang handbook followed Ball's earlier account insofar as it concerned itself with maintaining a high-quality standard for the product. But now it was also designed to create a full itinerary for an 18-incense-stick – or 12-hour – workday. Jiang Yaohua mapped out a schedule that maximised the amount of productive activity possible given the physical limits of his human workforce. Decades later, a social surveyor named Fan Hejun would visit the Huizhou tea districts and comment on the effects of this 18-stick workday. 'During that time,' he wrote, 'one day of roasting labour completely depletes all muscle strength.' Fan Hejun further observed that the incense sticks now burned to regulate the wages of hired workers: Taking four baskets as one shift, the skilled workers can earn four units of wages, with each unit worth [about 15 cents]. The unskilled workers who can only roast two baskets per shift only earn, in the span of 18 incense sticks, two units of wages. Within this action-packed schedule, the incense sticks were used to keep workers on task, leaving little room for the flexible attitude towards completing tasks seen in Ball's day.","question":"Fan Hejun's observations in the Huizhou tea districts support all of the following statements EXCEPT","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"With the incense sticks as a baseline, the managers rewarded workers based upon how many pounds of tea they could process in a given time.","B":"Under pressure to operate as long and efficiently as possible, managers in Huizhou pushed workers to the limits of their bodies, and beyond.","C":"Oversupply by Chinese tea producers combined with prices that had begun to tail off forced merchants to drive their workers very hard.","D":"Though the Huizhou merchants continued to burn old-fashioned incense timekeepers, the character of their application had changed."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Oversupply by Chinese tea producers combined with prices that had begun to tail off forced merchants to drive their workers very hard.","difficulty":"hard","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"premise_restatement","B":"premise_restatement","D":"premise_restatement"},"explanation":"Choice A is supported — refer to 'Taking four baskets as one shift, the skilled workers can earn four units of wages... The unskilled workers who can only roast two baskets per shift only earn two units of wages.' Workers are rewarded based on output within the timed framework. Choice B is supported — refer to 'Jiang Yaohua mapped out a schedule that maximised the amount of productive activity possible given the physical limits of his human workforce... one day of roasting labour completely depletes all muscle strength.' Choice C is CORRECT — this is not stated as such in the passage. Fan Hejun's observations do not mention oversupply or falling prices as reasons for harsh labour conditions. Choice D is supported — the handbook shows that the same incense sticks that once guided quality now rigidly regulated the workday and wages, showing a fundamental change in their application.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E3_006","exercise":"exercise_3","passage_id":"RC_E3_P2","passage":"Evidence that liberalism is undergoing its deepest crisis since the Great Depression surrounds us. At the core of the crisis is the steady rise in economic inequality in the United States. So, has liberalism as a philosophy of politics and economics failed? We need to be far more specific about the problems of contemporary capitalism. The United States adopted a particular strand of liberalism called \\"neoliberalism\\" at the behest of an alliance of politicians, donors, and business lobbies. To give their movement a popular face, the partners in this alliance propagated an ideology of national power, manliness, racial resentment, and anti-liberalism. Jacob Hacker and Paul Pierson call this movement \\"plutocratic populism.\\"\\n\\nEquivalent forms have gained momentum around the world, which in some countries has led to the subversion of pluralist democracy in the name of \\"ending the era of globalist liberalism,\\" as in Viktor Orbán's Hungary. Vladimir Putin's Russia exhibits a still more extreme form of anti-liberalism, as the regime has cultivated a cult of the strongman, messianic imperialism, and outright military aggression. These ideological amalgams are the twenty-first-century equivalents of mid-twentieth-century fascism. America's embrace of a right-wing economic agenda starting in the early 1980s is the main reason why we have seen a massive increase in wealth flowing to those wielding outsize policy influence.\\n\\nThe beneficiaries of this wealth aim to preserve it by keeping the government from protecting the public interest. Such practices are economically unproductive and inefficient, yielding \\"unearned income\\" known as \\"rents.\\" According to Joseph Stiglitz, rents can deepen economic inequality by concentrating financial benefits at the top, holding down incomes for those at the bottom or in the middle.\\n\\nSome economists and politicians, to be sure, continue to dismiss the notion that inequality is a problem. One camp contends that inequality is a \\"myth\\" because the tax system and social-safety net alleviate most of—if not all—the inequality arising from the labor market. Moreover, they consider the myth dangerous since it incites populist calls for redistribution. A second group argues that poverty, not inequality, is the real problem: Steven Pinker advances a theory that inequality is an abstraction of little real relevance to societies or individuals, whereas poverty and deprivation are genuine social ills that require our attention. A third position is to defend inequality. The economist Gregory Mankiw published an essay provocatively titled \\"Defending the One Percent.\\"\\n\\nBut these economists tend to confuse neoclassical models with how the economy actually works. In their world, there is no rent-seeking by the rich. Their corporations don't spend billions of dollars to secure beneficial tax rules and regulatory exemptions or to promote doctrines that antitrust regulation is tantamount to government overreach. Government, politics, rents—all of these factors are missing from the calculations of those who imagine pure market forces dominate the economy. And contrary to the view that inequality does not matter in itself, a substantial body of literature demonstrates that inequality affects people in ways that cannot be reduced to poverty. As to the claim that inequality is in fact quite low, the figures that are used to support that assertion rely on arbitrary and implausible counting methods, greatly overestimating incomes at the bottom and middle and underestimating incomes at the top.\\n\\nThe high level of inequality in the United States makes it an outlier among wealthy democracies; more incredibly, it somewhat resembles Russia in its extreme concentration of wealth at the top end of the income distribution. In terms of wealth, the top one percent of Americans saw a one-third increase in their total share of national wealth between 1978 and 2021, from 21.6 to 34.9 percent. Meanwhile, the wealth of the wealthiest one percent of Russians more than doubled between 1995 and 2021, rising from 21.5 percent to 47.6 percent.","question":"Which of the following could credibly replace the term 'plutocratic populism' mentioned in the first paragraph of the passage?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Reactionary rhetoric","B":"Antidemocratic populism","C":"Right-wing fearmongering","D":"Polarizing conservatism"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"Antidemocratic populism","difficulty":"hard","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"out_of_scope","C":"out_of_scope","D":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"The key to answering this question lies in 'the subversion of pluralist democracy' mentioned in the second paragraph as an equivalent form of plutocratic populism. Choice A is an inaccurate representation — reactionary rhetoric does not capture the antidemocratic and wealth-concentration aspects. Choice B is CORRECT — it captures both the populist appeal and the antidemocratic subversion of pluralist democracy that the passage associates with this movement. Choice C is an inaccurate representation — fearmongering is too narrow. Choice D is an inaccurate representation — conservatism is too broad and does not capture the democracy-subverting aspect.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E3_007","exercise":"exercise_3","passage_id":"RC_E3_P2","passage":"Evidence that liberalism is undergoing its deepest crisis since the Great Depression surrounds us. At the core of the crisis is the steady rise in economic inequality in the United States. So, has liberalism as a philosophy of politics and economics failed? We need to be far more specific about the problems of contemporary capitalism. The United States adopted a particular strand of liberalism called \\"neoliberalism\\" at the behest of an alliance of politicians, donors, and business lobbies. To give their movement a popular face, the partners in this alliance propagated an ideology of national power, manliness, racial resentment, and anti-liberalism. Jacob Hacker and Paul Pierson call this movement \\"plutocratic populism.\\"\\n\\nEquivalent forms have gained momentum around the world, which in some countries has led to the subversion of pluralist democracy in the name of \\"ending the era of globalist liberalism,\\" as in Viktor Orbán's Hungary. Vladimir Putin's Russia exhibits a still more extreme form of anti-liberalism, as the regime has cultivated a cult of the strongman, messianic imperialism, and outright military aggression. These ideological amalgams are the twenty-first-century equivalents of mid-twentieth-century fascism. America's embrace of a right-wing economic agenda starting in the early 1980s is the main reason why we have seen a massive increase in wealth flowing to those wielding outsize policy influence.\\n\\nThe beneficiaries of this wealth aim to preserve it by keeping the government from protecting the public interest. Such practices are economically unproductive and inefficient, yielding \\"unearned income\\" known as \\"rents.\\" According to Joseph Stiglitz, rents can deepen economic inequality by concentrating financial benefits at the top, holding down incomes for those at the bottom or in the middle.\\n\\nSome economists and politicians, to be sure, continue to dismiss the notion that inequality is a problem. One camp contends that inequality is a \\"myth\\" because the tax system and social-safety net alleviate most of—if not all—the inequality arising from the labor market. Moreover, they consider the myth dangerous since it incites populist calls for redistribution. A second group argues that poverty, not inequality, is the real problem: Steven Pinker advances a theory that inequality is an abstraction of little real relevance to societies or individuals, whereas poverty and deprivation are genuine social ills that require our attention. A third position is to defend inequality. The economist Gregory Mankiw published an essay provocatively titled \\"Defending the One Percent.\\"\\n\\nBut these economists tend to confuse neoclassical models with how the economy actually works. In their world, there is no rent-seeking by the rich. Their corporations don't spend billions of dollars to secure beneficial tax rules and regulatory exemptions or to promote doctrines that antitrust regulation is tantamount to government overreach. Government, politics, rents—all of these factors are missing from the calculations of those who imagine pure market forces dominate the economy. And contrary to the view that inequality does not matter in itself, a substantial body of literature demonstrates that inequality affects people in ways that cannot be reduced to poverty. As to the claim that inequality is in fact quite low, the figures that are used to support that assertion rely on arbitrary and implausible counting methods, greatly overestimating incomes at the bottom and middle and underestimating incomes at the top.\\n\\nThe high level of inequality in the United States makes it an outlier among wealthy democracies; more incredibly, it somewhat resembles Russia in its extreme concentration of wealth at the top end of the income distribution. In terms of wealth, the top one percent of Americans saw a one-third increase in their total share of national wealth between 1978 and 2021, from 21.6 to 34.9 percent. Meanwhile, the wealth of the wealthiest one percent of Russians more than doubled between 1995 and 2021, rising from 21.5 percent to 47.6 percent.","question":"All of the following exemplify 'Such practices' mentioned in the second paragraph EXCEPT","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Healthcare companies shift the cost of privatized healthcare services to patients and the public in order to pocket the profits.","B":"Meatpacking plants exploit underage children and migrants.","C":"Tax and regulatory policies punish the private-equity industry for sucking value out of the real economy.","D":"Government subsidizes financial institutions when they float bonds saddling poor communities with higher interest rates."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Tax and regulatory policies punish the private-equity industry for sucking value out of the real economy.","difficulty":"hard","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"premise_restatement","B":"premise_restatement","D":"premise_restatement"},"explanation":"'Such practices' refers to the beneficiaries of wealth keeping the government from protecting the public interest. Choice A exemplifies this — shifting costs to the public while pocketing profits is keeping government from protecting public interest. Choice B exemplifies this — exploitation of vulnerable workers represents the same dynamic. Choice C is CORRECT — 'Such practices' would be exemplified by tax and regulatory policies that encourage the private-equity industry to suck value out of the real economy. The option as stated says policies punish the industry, which is the opposite of what the passage describes. Choice D exemplifies this — government subsidizing institutions that harm poor communities is keeping government from protecting public interest.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E3_008","exercise":"exercise_3","passage_id":"RC_E3_P2","passage":"Evidence that liberalism is undergoing its deepest crisis since the Great Depression surrounds us. At the core of the crisis is the steady rise in economic inequality in the United States. So, has liberalism as a philosophy of politics and economics failed? We need to be far more specific about the problems of contemporary capitalism. The United States adopted a particular strand of liberalism called \\"neoliberalism\\" at the behest of an alliance of politicians, donors, and business lobbies. To give their movement a popular face, the partners in this alliance propagated an ideology of national power, manliness, racial resentment, and anti-liberalism. Jacob Hacker and Paul Pierson call this movement \\"plutocratic populism.\\"\\n\\nEquivalent forms have gained momentum around the world, which in some countries has led to the subversion of pluralist democracy in the name of \\"ending the era of globalist liberalism,\\" as in Viktor Orbán's Hungary. Vladimir Putin's Russia exhibits a still more extreme form of anti-liberalism, as the regime has cultivated a cult of the strongman, messianic imperialism, and outright military aggression. These ideological amalgams are the twenty-first-century equivalents of mid-twentieth-century fascism. America's embrace of a right-wing economic agenda starting in the early 1980s is the main reason why we have seen a massive increase in wealth flowing to those wielding outsize policy influence.\\n\\nThe beneficiaries of this wealth aim to preserve it by keeping the government from protecting the public interest. Such practices are economically unproductive and inefficient, yielding \\"unearned income\\" known as \\"rents.\\" According to Joseph Stiglitz, rents can deepen economic inequality by concentrating financial benefits at the top, holding down incomes for those at the bottom or in the middle.\\n\\nSome economists and politicians, to be sure, continue to dismiss the notion that inequality is a problem. One camp contends that inequality is a \\"myth\\" because the tax system and social-safety net alleviate most of—if not all—the inequality arising from the labor market. Moreover, they consider the myth dangerous since it incites populist calls for redistribution. A second group argues that poverty, not inequality, is the real problem: Steven Pinker advances a theory that inequality is an abstraction of little real relevance to societies or individuals, whereas poverty and deprivation are genuine social ills that require our attention. A third position is to defend inequality. The economist Gregory Mankiw published an essay provocatively titled \\"Defending the One Percent.\\"\\n\\nBut these economists tend to confuse neoclassical models with how the economy actually works. In their world, there is no rent-seeking by the rich. Their corporations don't spend billions of dollars to secure beneficial tax rules and regulatory exemptions or to promote doctrines that antitrust regulation is tantamount to government overreach. Government, politics, rents—all of these factors are missing from the calculations of those who imagine pure market forces dominate the economy. And contrary to the view that inequality does not matter in itself, a substantial body of literature demonstrates that inequality affects people in ways that cannot be reduced to poverty. As to the claim that inequality is in fact quite low, the figures that are used to support that assertion rely on arbitrary and implausible counting methods, greatly overestimating incomes at the bottom and middle and underestimating incomes at the top.\\n\\nThe high level of inequality in the United States makes it an outlier among wealthy democracies; more incredibly, it somewhat resembles Russia in its extreme concentration of wealth at the top end of the income distribution. In terms of wealth, the top one percent of Americans saw a one-third increase in their total share of national wealth between 1978 and 2021, from 21.6 to 34.9 percent. Meanwhile, the wealth of the wealthiest one percent of Russians more than doubled between 1995 and 2021, rising from 21.5 percent to 47.6 percent.","question":"All of the following arguments are cited by 'Some economists and politicians … (who) … continue to dismiss the notion that inequality is a problem' EXCEPT","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Inequality is non-existent because the tax system and social-safety net compensate for inequality arising from the labor market.","B":"There are grounds for taxing the wealthy for redistribution purposes if their wealth comes at the expense of other people's labor that is unfairly or insufficiently rewarded.","C":"Combating poverty is more important than reducing inequality.","D":"Governments should focus less on taking actions that could inhibit top earners."},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"There are grounds for taxing the wealthy for redistribution purposes if their wealth comes at the expense of other people's labor that is unfairly or insufficiently rewarded.","difficulty":"hard","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"premise_restatement","C":"premise_restatement","D":"premise_restatement"},"explanation":"Choice A is a cited argument — refer to 'inequality is a myth because the tax system and social-safety net alleviate most of the inequality arising from the labor market.' Choice B is CORRECT — this is the opposite of what the economists cited argue. They consider the myth of inequality dangerous since it incites populist calls for redistribution, meaning they are against redistribution, not for it. Choice C is a cited argument — refer to 'inequality is an abstraction of little real relevance to societies or individuals, whereas poverty and deprivation are genuine social ills.' Choice D is a cited argument — refer to 'Defending the One Percent', which implies caution about populist reactions that focus too much on the super-rich.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E3_009","exercise":"exercise_3","passage_id":"RC_E3_P2","passage":"Evidence that liberalism is undergoing its deepest crisis since the Great Depression surrounds us. At the core of the crisis is the steady rise in economic inequality in the United States. So, has liberalism as a philosophy of politics and economics failed? We need to be far more specific about the problems of contemporary capitalism. The United States adopted a particular strand of liberalism called \\"neoliberalism\\" at the behest of an alliance of politicians, donors, and business lobbies. To give their movement a popular face, the partners in this alliance propagated an ideology of national power, manliness, racial resentment, and anti-liberalism. Jacob Hacker and Paul Pierson call this movement \\"plutocratic populism.\\"\\n\\nEquivalent forms have gained momentum around the world, which in some countries has led to the subversion of pluralist democracy in the name of \\"ending the era of globalist liberalism,\\" as in Viktor Orbán's Hungary. Vladimir Putin's Russia exhibits a still more extreme form of anti-liberalism, as the regime has cultivated a cult of the strongman, messianic imperialism, and outright military aggression. These ideological amalgams are the twenty-first-century equivalents of mid-twentieth-century fascism. America's embrace of a right-wing economic agenda starting in the early 1980s is the main reason why we have seen a massive increase in wealth flowing to those wielding outsize policy influence.\\n\\nThe beneficiaries of this wealth aim to preserve it by keeping the government from protecting the public interest. Such practices are economically unproductive and inefficient, yielding \\"unearned income\\" known as \\"rents.\\" According to Joseph Stiglitz, rents can deepen economic inequality by concentrating financial benefits at the top, holding down incomes for those at the bottom or in the middle.\\n\\nSome economists and politicians, to be sure, continue to dismiss the notion that inequality is a problem. One camp contends that inequality is a \\"myth\\" because the tax system and social-safety net alleviate most of—if not all—the inequality arising from the labor market. Moreover, they consider the myth dangerous since it incites populist calls for redistribution. A second group argues that poverty, not inequality, is the real problem: Steven Pinker advances a theory that inequality is an abstraction of little real relevance to societies or individuals, whereas poverty and deprivation are genuine social ills that require our attention. A third position is to defend inequality. The economist Gregory Mankiw published an essay provocatively titled \\"Defending the One Percent.\\"\\n\\nBut these economists tend to confuse neoclassical models with how the economy actually works. In their world, there is no rent-seeking by the rich. Their corporations don't spend billions of dollars to secure beneficial tax rules and regulatory exemptions or to promote doctrines that antitrust regulation is tantamount to government overreach. Government, politics, rents—all of these factors are missing from the calculations of those who imagine pure market forces dominate the economy. And contrary to the view that inequality does not matter in itself, a substantial body of literature demonstrates that inequality affects people in ways that cannot be reduced to poverty. As to the claim that inequality is in fact quite low, the figures that are used to support that assertion rely on arbitrary and implausible counting methods, greatly overestimating incomes at the bottom and middle and underestimating incomes at the top.\\n\\nThe high level of inequality in the United States makes it an outlier among wealthy democracies; more incredibly, it somewhat resembles Russia in its extreme concentration of wealth at the top end of the income distribution. In terms of wealth, the top one percent of Americans saw a one-third increase in their total share of national wealth between 1978 and 2021, from 21.6 to 34.9 percent. Meanwhile, the wealth of the wealthiest one percent of Russians more than doubled between 1995 and 2021, rising from 21.5 percent to 47.6 percent.","question":"Which of the following statements strengthen the view that 'inequality affects people in ways that cannot be reduced to poverty'?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"The absolute poor have nothing that can be described as wealth.","B":"The rise in overall wealth inequality is due less to very wealthy people's success than to the middle class's failures.","C":"Inequality makes people feel poor and act poor, even when they're not.","D":"The healthiest and happiest societies are those with the lowest levels of inequality."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Inequality makes people feel poor and act poor, even when they're not.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"out_of_scope","B":"out_of_scope","D":"correct_but_unstated"},"explanation":"Choice A describes the state of the absolute poor and does not strengthen the argument that inequality impacts people beyond poverty. Choice B is a contestable matter of opinion and does not directly support the argument. Choice C is CORRECT — this directly supports the argument that inequality impacts people in ways that cannot be reduced to poverty, by showing that the psychological and behavioural effects of inequality exist independently of actual poverty. Choice D describes a satisfactory state of affairs but does not directly relate to the specific argument that inequality affects people beyond poverty.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E3_010","exercise":"exercise_3","passage_id":"RC_E3_P2","passage":"Evidence that liberalism is undergoing its deepest crisis since the Great Depression surrounds us. At the core of the crisis is the steady rise in economic inequality in the United States. So, has liberalism as a philosophy of politics and economics failed? We need to be far more specific about the problems of contemporary capitalism. The United States adopted a particular strand of liberalism called \\"neoliberalism\\" at the behest of an alliance of politicians, donors, and business lobbies. To give their movement a popular face, the partners in this alliance propagated an ideology of national power, manliness, racial resentment, and anti-liberalism. Jacob Hacker and Paul Pierson call this movement \\"plutocratic populism.\\"\\n\\nEquivalent forms have gained momentum around the world, which in some countries has led to the subversion of pluralist democracy in the name of \\"ending the era of globalist liberalism,\\" as in Viktor Orbán's Hungary. Vladimir Putin's Russia exhibits a still more extreme form of anti-liberalism, as the regime has cultivated a cult of the strongman, messianic imperialism, and outright military aggression. These ideological amalgams are the twenty-first-century equivalents of mid-twentieth-century fascism. America's embrace of a right-wing economic agenda starting in the early 1980s is the main reason why we have seen a massive increase in wealth flowing to those wielding outsize policy influence.\\n\\nThe beneficiaries of this wealth aim to preserve it by keeping the government from protecting the public interest. Such practices are economically unproductive and inefficient, yielding \\"unearned income\\" known as \\"rents.\\" According to Joseph Stiglitz, rents can deepen economic inequality by concentrating financial benefits at the top, holding down incomes for those at the bottom or in the middle.\\n\\nSome economists and politicians, to be sure, continue to dismiss the notion that inequality is a problem. At the core of the crisis is the steady rise in economic inequality in the United States. One camp contends that inequality is a \\"myth\\" because the tax system and social-safety net alleviate most of—if not all—the inequality arising from the labor market. Moreover, they consider the myth dangerous since it incites populist calls for redistribution. A second group argues that poverty, not inequality, is the real problem: Steven Pinker advances a theory that inequality is an abstraction of little real relevance to societies or individuals, whereas poverty and deprivation are genuine social ills that require our attention. A third position is to defend inequality. The economist Gregory Mankiw published an essay provocatively titled \\"Defending the One Percent.\\"\\n\\nBut these economists tend to confuse neoclassical models with how the economy actually works. In their world, there is no rent-seeking by the rich. Their corporations don't spend billions of dollars to secure beneficial tax rules and regulatory exemptions or to promote doctrines that antitrust regulation is tantamount to government overreach. Government, politics, rents—all of these factors are missing from the calculations of those who imagine pure market forces dominate the economy. And contrary to the view that inequality does not matter in itself, a substantial body of literature demonstrates that inequality affects people in ways that cannot be reduced to poverty. As to the claim that inequality is in fact quite low, the figures that are used to support that assertion rely on arbitrary and implausible counting methods, greatly overestimating incomes at the bottom and middle and underestimating incomes at the top.\\n\\nThe high level of inequality in the United States makes it an outlier among wealthy democracies; more incredibly, it somewhat resembles Russia in its extreme concentration of wealth at the top end of the income distribution. In terms of wealth, the top one percent of Americans saw a one-third increase in their total share of national wealth between 1978 and 2021, from 21.6 to 34.9 percent. Meanwhile, the wealth of the wealthiest one percent of Russians more than doubled between 1995 and 2021, rising from 21.5 percent to 47.6 percent.","question":"According to the last paragraph, which of the following statements are true?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"In the United States and Russia, large and powerful firms charge prices that far exceed their production costs, allowing them to raise executive pay and generate big returns for their owners without raising employee wages commensurately.","B":"In both Russian and American economies, there appears to be a divergence between profitability and productivity—an indicator of high rent-seeking.","C":"Both in Russia and in the United States, the most profitable enterprises were the least productive, whereas the most productive enterprises were the least profitable.","D":"The proportion of national wealth controlled by the top one percent in both the United States and Russia has risen significantly over time, indicating a growing concentration of wealth in both countries."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"The proportion of national wealth controlled by the top one percent in both the United States and Russia has risen significantly over time, indicating a growing concentration of wealth in both countries.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"out_of_scope","B":"out_of_scope","C":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"Choice A is not stated in the passage. Choice B is not stated in the passage. Choice C is not stated in the passage. Choice D is CORRECT — refer to 'the top one percent of Americans saw a one-third increase in their total share of national wealth between 1978 and 2021, from 21.6 to 34.9 percent. Meanwhile, the wealth of the wealthiest one percent of Russians more than doubled between 1995 and 2021, rising from 21.5 percent to 47.6 percent.' Both figures show significant growth in wealth concentration at the top.","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"RC_E3_011","exercise":"exercise_3","passage_id":"RC_E3_P3","passage":"When science-fiction worlds introduce robots that look and behave like people, sooner or later those worlds' inhabitants confront the question of robot self-awareness. If a machine is built to truly mimic a human, its 'brain' must be complex enough not only to process information as ours do, but also to achieve certain types of abstract thinking that make us human. This includes recognition of our 'selves' and our place to the world, a state known as consciousness.\\n\\nBut what exactly might 'consciousness' mean for Artificial Intelligence (AI) in the real world, and how close is AI to reaching that goal? Philosophers have described consciousness as having a unique sense of self coupled with an awareness of what's going on around you. Neuro-scientists have offered their own perspective on how consciousness might be quantified, through analysis of a person's brain activity as it integrates and interprets sensory data.\\n\\nHowever, applying those rules to AI is tricky. In some ways, the process abilities of AI are not unlike those that take place in human brains. Sophisticated AI systems use a process called deep learning to solve computational tasks quickly, using networks of layered algorithms that communicate with each other to solve more and more complex problems. It's a strategy very similar to that of our own brains, where information speeds across connections between neurons. In a neural network, deep learning enables AI to teach itself how to identify a disease, or win a strategy game against the best human player in the world.\\n\\nBut to accomplish these feats, any neural network still relies on a human programmer setting the tasks and selecting the data for it to learn from. Consciousness for AI would mean that neural networks could make those initial choices themselves, 'deviating from the programmers' intentions and doing their own thing'.\\n\\nOne of the pitfalls for machines becoming self-aware is that consciousness in humans is not well-defined enough, which would make it difficult, if not impossible, for programmers to replicate such a state in algorithms for AI. Scientists defined three levels of human consciousness, based on the computation that happens in the brain. The first, which they labeled 'C0,' represents calculations that happen without our knowledge, such as during facial recognition, and most AI functions at this level. The second level, 'C1,' involves a so-called 'global' awareness of information – in other words, actively sifting and evaluating quantities of data to make an informed, deliberate choice in response to specific circumstances. Self-awareness emerges in the third level 'C2,' in which individuals recognize and correct mistakes and investigate the unknown.\\n\\nOnce scientists can spell out in computational terms what the differences may be in humans between consciousness and unconsciousness, coding that into computers may not be that hard. To a certain extent, some types of AI can evaluate their actions and correct them responsively – a component of the C2 level of human consciousness. But we cannot expect to meet self-aware AI anytime soon.","question":"The author mentions neuro-scientists in the second paragraph to indicate that","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"consciousness is hard to achieve in relation to AI because of technical limitations.","B":"the definition of consciousness is inadequate, given the need for accuracy to incorporate it in AI.","C":"the human brain has substantial difficulty in integrating and interpreting sensory data.","D":"the attempt for the quantification of consciousness may present a new outlook in relation to AI."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"the attempt for the quantification of consciousness may present a new outlook in relation to AI.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"correct_but_unstated","B":"correct_but_unstated","C":"contradicts_passage"},"explanation":"The last sentence of the second paragraph states that neuro-scientists have offered their own perspective on how consciousness might be quantified through brain activity analysis. This adds a new perspective in relation to the application of consciousness to AI. Choice A is incorrect because though this is a true statement, this is what the mentioning of neuro-scientists is designed to imply, not the primary purpose of the mention. Choice B is incorrect because the purpose of mentioning the scientists is not to question the adequacy of the definition. Choice C is incorrect because this statement is factually wrong — the passage says the brain integrates and interprets sensory data, not that it has difficulty doing so. Choice D is CORRECT as per the reason given above.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E3_012","exercise":"exercise_3","passage_id":"RC_E3_P3","passage":"When science-fiction worlds introduce robots that look and behave like people, sooner or later those worlds' inhabitants confront the question of robot self-awareness. If a machine is built to truly mimic a human, its 'brain' must be complex enough not only to process information as ours do, but also to achieve certain types of abstract thinking that make us human. This includes recognition of our 'selves' and our place to the world, a state known as consciousness.\\n\\nBut what exactly might 'consciousness' mean for Artificial Intelligence (AI) in the real world, and how close is AI to reaching that goal? Philosophers have described consciousness as having a unique sense of self coupled with an awareness of what's going on around you. Neuro-scientists have offered their own perspective on how consciousness might be quantified, through analysis of a person's brain activity as it integrates and interprets sensory data.\\n\\nHowever, applying those rules to AI is tricky. In some ways, the process abilities of AI are not unlike those that take place in human brains. Sophisticated AI systems use a process called deep learning to solve computational tasks quickly, using networks of layered algorithms that communicate with each other to solve more and more complex problems. It's a strategy very similar to that of our own brains, where information speeds across connections between neurons. In a neural network, deep learning enables AI to teach itself how to identify a disease, or win a strategy game against the best human player in the world.\\n\\nBut to accomplish these feats, any neural network still relies on a human programmer setting the tasks and selecting the data for it to learn from. Consciousness for AI would mean that neural networks could make those initial choices themselves, 'deviating from the programmers' intentions and doing their own thing'.\\n\\nOne of the pitfalls for machines becoming self-aware is that consciousness in humans is not well-defined enough, which would make it difficult, if not impossible, for programmers to replicate such a state in algorithms for AI. Scientists defined three levels of human consciousness, based on the computation that happens in the brain. The first, which they labeled 'C0,' represents calculations that happen without our knowledge, such as during facial recognition, and most AI functions at this level. The second level, 'C1,' involves a so-called 'global' awareness of information – in other words, actively sifting and evaluating quantities of data to make an informed, deliberate choice in response to specific circumstances. Self-awareness emerges in the third level 'C2,' in which individuals recognize and correct mistakes and investigate the unknown.\\n\\nOnce scientists can spell out in computational terms what the differences may be in humans between consciousness and unconsciousness, coding that into computers may not be that hard. To a certain extent, some types of AI can evaluate their actions and correct them responsively – a component of the C2 level of human consciousness. But we cannot expect to meet self-aware AI anytime soon.","question":"According to the passage, making machines self-aware is difficult because","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"for the three levels of human consciousness, scientists developed algorithms only for the first two levels.","B":"for want of clear-cut definition, development of algorithms to serve the purpose is difficult.","C":"for neuro-scientists, the distinction between consciousness and unconsciousness is not clear.","D":"for computers, self-evaluation has become possible to some extent."},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"for want of clear-cut definition, development of algorithms to serve the purpose is difficult.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"out_of_scope","C":"correct_but_unstated","D":"contradicts_passage"},"explanation":"As per the first sentence of the fifth paragraph, the concept of self-awareness is not clearly defined and so algorithms required to develop self-awareness in computers are difficult to develop. Choice A is incorrect because per the third sentence of the fifth paragraph, AI can undertake actions related to C0, but no information is given about whether algorithms for C1 level have been developed. Choice B is CORRECT as per the reasons given above. Choice C is incorrect because it is the spelling out of the difference in computational terms that is preventing machines from becoming self-aware, not a lack of understanding by neuro-scientists per se. Choice D is incorrect because if self-evaluation had been fully achieved, AI would have already become self-aware.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E3_013","exercise":"exercise_3","passage_id":"RC_E3_P3","passage":"When science-fiction worlds introduce robots that look and behave like people, sooner or later those worlds' inhabitants confront the question of robot self-awareness. If a machine is built to truly mimic a human, its 'brain' must be complex enough not only to process information as ours do, but also to achieve certain types of abstract thinking that make us human. This includes recognition of our 'selves' and our place to the world, a state known as consciousness.\\n\\nBut what exactly might 'consciousness' mean for Artificial Intelligence (AI) in the real world, and how close is AI to reaching that goal? Philosophers have described consciousness as having a unique sense of self coupled with an awareness of what's going on around you. Neuro-scientists have offered their own perspective on how consciousness might be quantified, through analysis of a person's brain activity as it integrates and interprets sensory data.\\n\\nHowever, applying those rules to AI is tricky. In some ways, the process abilities of AI are not unlike those that take place in human brains. Sophisticated AI systems use a process called deep learning to solve computational tasks quickly, using networks of layered algorithms that communicate with each other to solve more and more complex problems. It's a strategy very similar to that of our own brains, where information speeds across connections between neurons. In a neural network, deep learning enables AI to teach itself how to identify a disease, or win a strategy game against the best human player in the world.\\n\\nBut to accomplish these feats, any neural network still relies on a human programmer setting the tasks and selecting the data for it to learn from. Consciousness for AI would mean that neural networks could make those initial choices themselves, 'deviating from the programmers' intentions and doing their own thing'.\\n\\nOne of the pitfalls for machines becoming self-aware is that consciousness in humans is not well-defined enough, which would make it difficult, if not impossible, for programmers to replicate such a state in algorithms for AI. Scientists defined three levels of human consciousness, based on the computation that happens in the brain. The first, which they labeled 'C0,' represents calculations that happen without our knowledge, such as during facial recognition, and most AI functions at this level. The second level, 'C1,' involves a so-called 'global' awareness of information – in other words, actively sifting and evaluating quantities of data to make an informed, deliberate choice in response to specific circumstances. Self-awareness emerges in the third level 'C2,' in which individuals recognize and correct mistakes and investigate the unknown.\\n\\nOnce scientists can spell out in computational terms what the differences may be in humans between consciousness and unconsciousness, coding that into computers may not be that hard. To a certain extent, some types of AI can evaluate their actions and correct them responsively – a component of the C2 level of human consciousness. But we cannot expect to meet self-aware AI anytime soon.","question":"Which of the following can be inferred based on the information given above?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Computer scientists have focused too much on the definition of consciousness, at the expense of other important aspects to be considered.","B":"AI cannot become self-aware because development of algorithms related to all components of C2 level of consciousness is not a realistic possibility in the near future.","C":"Exploration of the unknown is beyond the reach of the human consciousness.","D":"Deep learning in humans and that in AI are radically different."},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"AI cannot become self-aware because development of algorithms related to all components of C2 level of consciousness is not a realistic possibility in the near future.","difficulty":"hard","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"out_of_scope","C":"contradicts_passage","D":"contradicts_passage"},"explanation":"As per the last two sentences of the passage, some types of AI can self-evaluate (a component of C2) but we cannot achieve full self-aware AI anytime soon. This implies that all components of C2 level of consciousness cannot be achieved in the near future. Choice A is incorrect because the passage does not support the view that scientists concentrated 'too much' on the definition. Choice B is CORRECT as per the reasons given. Choice C is incorrect because exploration of the unknown is said to be possible at C2 level of consciousness. Choice D is incorrect because the second and third sentences of the third paragraph explicitly state that AI's process abilities are not unlike those in human brains.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E3_014","exercise":"exercise_3","passage_id":"RC_E3_P3","passage":"When science-fiction worlds introduce robots that look and behave like people, sooner or later those worlds' inhabitants confront the question of robot self-awareness. If a machine is built to truly mimic a human, its 'brain' must be complex enough not only to process information as ours do, but also to achieve certain types of abstract thinking that make us human. This includes recognition of our 'selves' and our place to the world, a state known as consciousness.\\n\\nBut what exactly might 'consciousness' mean for Artificial Intelligence (AI) in the real world, and how close is AI to reaching that goal? Philosophers have described consciousness as having a unique sense of self coupled with an awareness of what's going on around you. Neuro-scientists have offered their own perspective on how consciousness might be quantified, through analysis of a person's brain activity as it integrates and interprets sensory data.\\n\\nHowever, applying those rules to AI is tricky. In some ways, the process abilities of AI are not unlike those that take place in human brains. Sophisticated AI systems use a process called deep learning to solve computational tasks quickly, using networks of layered algorithms that communicate with each other to solve more and more complex problems. It's a strategy very similar to that of our own brains, where information speeds across connections between neurons. In a neural network, deep learning enables AI to teach itself how to identify a disease, or win a strategy game against the best human player in the world.\\n\\nBut to accomplish these feats, any neural network still relies on a human programmer setting the tasks and selecting the data for it to learn from. Consciousness for AI would mean that neural networks could make those initial choices themselves, 'deviating from the programmers' intentions and doing their own thing'.\\n\\nOne of the pitfalls for machines becoming self-aware is that consciousness in humans is not well-defined enough, which would make it difficult, if not impossible, for programmers to replicate such a state in algorithms for AI. Scientists defined three levels of human consciousness, based on the computation that happens in the brain. The first, which they labeled 'C0,' represents calculations that happen without our knowledge, such as during facial recognition, and most AI functions at this level. The second level, 'C1,' involves a so-called 'global' awareness of information – in other words, actively sifting and evaluating quantities of data to make an informed, deliberate choice in response to specific circumstances. Self-awareness emerges in the third level 'C2,' in which individuals recognize and correct mistakes and investigate the unknown.\\n\\nOnce scientists can spell out in computational terms what the differences may be in humans between consciousness and unconsciousness, coding that into computers may not be that hard. To a certain extent, some types of AI can evaluate their actions and correct them responsively – a component of the C2 level of human consciousness. But we cannot expect to meet self-aware AI anytime soon.","question":"According to the passage, all of the following are attributes of the state of consciousness EXCEPT:","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"some cognitive actions happen without any conscious effort and knowledge.","B":"in higher levels of consciousness, self-evaluation and self-correction are possible.","C":"its processing abilities are radically different from the ways AI is likely to process.","D":"perception and decision making are interrelated in the state of consciousness."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"its processing abilities are radically different from the ways AI is likely to process.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"premise_restatement","B":"premise_restatement","D":"premise_restatement"},"explanation":"Choice A is a true attribute — per the third sentence of the fifth paragraph, C0 represents calculations that happen without our knowledge, such as facial recognition. Choice B is a true attribute — per the last sentence of the fifth paragraph, C2 involves recognising and correcting mistakes. Choice C is CORRECT because this is an incorrect statement — per the second sentence of the third paragraph, the process abilities of AI are not unlike those that take place in human brains, meaning they are similar, not radically different. Choice D is a true attribute — per the fourth sentence of the fifth paragraph, C1 involves actively sifting and evaluating data to make an informed, deliberate choice, linking perception and decision making.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E3_015","exercise":"exercise_3","passage_id":"RC_E3_P3","passage":"When science-fiction worlds introduce robots that look and behave like people, sooner or later those worlds' inhabitants confront the question of robot self-awareness. If a machine is built to truly mimic a human, its 'brain' must be complex enough not only to process information as ours do, but also to achieve certain types of abstract thinking that make us human. This includes recognition of our 'selves' and our place to the world, a state known as consciousness.\\n\\nBut what exactly might 'consciousness' mean for Artificial Intelligence (AI) in the real world, and how close is AI to reaching that goal? Philosophers have described consciousness as having a unique sense of self coupled with an awareness of what's going on around you. Neuro-scientists have offered their own perspective on how consciousness might be quantified, through analysis of a person's brain activity as it integrates and interprets sensory data.\\n\\nHowever, applying those rules to AI is tricky. In some ways, the process abilities of AI are not unlike those that take place in human brains. Sophisticated AI systems use a process called deep learning to solve computational tasks quickly, using networks of layered algorithms that communicate with each other to solve more and more complex problems. It's a strategy very similar to that of our own brains, where information speeds across connections between neurons. In a neural network, deep learning enables AI to teach itself how to identify a disease, or win a strategy game against the best human player in the world.\\n\\nBut to accomplish these feats, any neural network still relies on a human programmer setting the tasks and selecting the data for it to learn from. Consciousness for AI would mean that neural networks could make those initial choices themselves, 'deviating from the programmers' intentions and doing their own thing'.\\n\\nOne of the pitfalls for machines becoming self-aware is that consciousness in humans is not well-defined enough, which would make it difficult, if not impossible, for programmers to replicate such a state in algorithms for AI. Scientists defined three levels of human consciousness, based on the computation that happens in the brain. The first, which they labeled 'C0,' represents calculations that happen without our knowledge, such as during facial recognition, and most AI functions at this level. The second level, 'C1,' involves a so-called 'global' awareness of information – in other words, actively sifting and evaluating quantities of data to make an informed, deliberate choice in response to specific circumstances. Self-awareness emerges in the third level 'C2,' in which individuals recognize and correct mistakes and investigate the unknown.\\n\\nOnce scientists can spell out in computational terms what the differences may be in humans between consciousness and unconsciousness, coding that into computers may not be that hard. To a certain extent, some types of AI can evaluate their actions and correct them responsively – a component of the C2 level of human consciousness. But we cannot expect to meet self-aware AI anytime soon.","question":"Which of the following best describes the function of the second paragraph in the context of the whole passage?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"It raises the questions that point out the obstacles needed to overcome in order to achieve fully functional AI.","B":"It raises some questions that are pertinent to bring out the difference between the philosophical and scientific perspectives of consciousness.","C":"It points out shortcomings that are prevalent in the academic disciplines discussed in the passage.","D":"It raises questions that point out a demerit of considering sensory data, which is essential for AI."},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"It raises the questions that point out the obstacles needed to overcome in order to achieve fully functional AI.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"B":"correct_but_unstated","C":"out_of_scope","D":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"The second paragraph mentions the question of consciousness in relation to realization of AI. These questions are answered to some extent in the later part of the passage. Choice A is CORRECT as per the reasons given above — the questions about what consciousness means for AI and how close AI is to reaching that goal point out the obstacles to be overcome. Choice B is incorrect because the questions are in relation to AI, not in relation to the differences between the two perspectives mentioned. Choice C is incorrect because the passage is not about academic disciplines. Choice D is incorrect because the primary idea of the passage is not sensory data.","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"RC_E3_016","exercise":"exercise_3","passage_id":"RC_E3_P4","passage":"Why do lions live in groups? The traditional explanation for lion sociality has been cooperative hunting, but recent research shows that they do not hunt as cooperatively as believed. In fact, lions only hunt cooperatively whenever required. If their best chance for meal is a large dangerous prey like a Cape buffalo, lions certainly do pull together. But if the prey is relatively easy for a singleton to capture, the rest of the pride is more likely to cheer on their hunting companion rather than join the hunt. Hence, we often see a lone female pursuing a warthog while the rest of the pride merely watches.\\n\\nConsequently, individual lions don't always feed better in larger groups. Food availability being highly seasonal, during the 'lean season', solitary females feed just as well as individuals in groups of five females; but groups of two to four females suffer lower food intakes. The evolution of sociality would have required a strong advantage for ancestral lions to start living in pairs or trios, so group hunting is not the answer.\\n\\nNursery groups serve as a defensive formation. After hiding their cubs for the first few weeks of life, mothers bring them to the rest of the pride and nursing mothers become almost inseparable for the next two years. But the members of nursery groups suffer lower feeding success, so the behavior does not derive from any advantage of group foraging.\\n\\nOutside males are always looking for mating opportunities. If they encounter a mother with small cubs, they will kill the cubs to induce her to return to mating condition. Resident males patrol the edges of the pride territory, and outside males sometimes encounter the nursery group unaccompanied. Cubs in a nursery group are far more likely to survive these encounters than cubs raised by singleton mothers. Groups of mothers can successfully counter-attack the invading males, whereas a female has no chance one-on-one against a fully grown male. Thus, nursery group formation clearly increases female reproductive success. However, most other felids, such as leopards, tigers, etc., also show similar infanticide by incoming males. So, defense against infanticide doesn't explain why lions are the only social cats.","question":"The primary purpose of the passage is to","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"explain the reasons for evolution of lion cooperative hunting.","B":"illustrate the feeding advantage for cubs in lion societies.","C":"question the traditional explanation for lion sociality.","D":"compare sociality in lions and other cat families."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"question the traditional explanation for lion sociality.","difficulty":"easy","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"out_of_scope","B":"contradicts_passage","D":"correct_but_unstated"},"explanation":"The passage raises the question of lions' sociality, gives the traditional explanation for that and goes on to say how that explanation cannot explain the evolution of lion sociality. Choice A is incorrect because, though it hints at cooperative hunting in the second paragraph, the primary purpose is to question the traditional explanation. Choice B is incorrect because the lions' sociality is not said to be advantageous — in fact, cubs in nursery groups are actually undernourished. Choice C is CORRECT as per the reasons given above. Choice D is incorrect because this comparison, given only in the last paragraph, is a minor point.","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"RC_E3_017","exercise":"exercise_3","passage_id":"RC_E3_P4","passage":"Why do lions live in groups? The traditional explanation for lion sociality has been cooperative hunting, but recent research shows that they do not hunt as cooperatively as believed. In fact, lions only hunt cooperatively whenever required. If their best chance for meal is a large dangerous prey like a Cape buffalo, lions certainly do pull together. But if the prey is relatively easy for a singleton to capture, the rest of the pride is more likely to cheer on their hunting companion rather than join the hunt. Hence, we often see a lone female pursuing a warthog while the rest of the pride merely watches.\\n\\nConsequently, individual lions don't always feed better in larger groups. Food availability being highly seasonal, during the 'lean season', solitary females feed just as well as individuals in groups of five females; but groups of two to four females suffer lower food intakes. The evolution of sociality would have required a strong advantage for ancestral lions to start living in pairs or trios, so group hunting is not the answer.\\n\\nNursery groups serve as a defensive formation. After hiding their cubs for the first few weeks of life, mothers bring them to the rest of the pride and nursing mothers become almost inseparable for the next two years. But the members of nursery groups suffer lower feeding success, so the behavior does not derive from any advantage of group foraging.\\n\\nOutside males are always looking for mating opportunities. If they encounter a mother with small cubs, they will kill the cubs to induce her to return to mating condition. Resident males patrol the edges of the pride territory, and outside males sometimes encounter the nursery group unaccompanied. Cubs in a nursery group are far more likely to survive these encounters than cubs raised by singleton mothers. Groups of mothers can successfully counter-attack the invading males, whereas a female has no chance one-on-one against a fully grown male. Thus, nursery group formation clearly increases female reproductive success. However, most other felids, such as leopards, tigers, etc., also show similar infanticide by incoming males. So, defense against infanticide doesn't explain why lions are the only social cats.","question":"Which of the following, if true, would most undermine the author's rejection of group foraging as an advantage of sociality found in lions?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"The cubs raised individually are far less successful in feeding than those raised in nursery groups.","B":"When female lions do not group their cubs in nursery groups, they give birth to more number of cubs than when nursery groups are formed.","C":"Cubs raised in nursery groups have a stronger inclination to form a pride than those raised individually.","D":"More female lions are found when they tend to encourage nursery groups than when they do not encourage nursery groups."},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"The cubs raised individually are far less successful in feeding than those raised in nursery groups.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"B":"out_of_scope","C":"out_of_scope","D":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"The author argues that members of nursery groups suffer lower feeding success and so sociality is not advantageous for foraging. The correct option should prove this rejection is wrong. Choice A is CORRECT — if cubs raised individually are far less successful in feeding than those raised in nursery groups, then being in a group is advantageous for feeding, which directly undermines the author's rejection of group foraging as an advantage. Choice B is incorrect because this is not related to group foraging success. Choice C is incorrect because this inclination to form a pride is not related to foraging success. Choice D is incorrect because the number of females is not related to foraging success of cubs raised in nursery groups.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E3_018","exercise":"exercise_3","passage_id":"RC_E3_P4","passage":"Why do lions live in groups? The traditional explanation for lion sociality has been cooperative hunting, but recent research shows that they do not hunt as cooperatively as believed. In fact, lions only hunt cooperatively whenever required. If their best chance for meal is a large dangerous prey like a Cape buffalo, lions certainly do pull together. But if the prey is relatively easy for a singleton to capture, the rest of the pride is more likely to cheer on their hunting companion rather than join the hunt. Hence, we often see a lone female pursuing a warthog while the rest of the pride merely watches.\\n\\nConsequently, individual lions don't always feed better in larger groups. Food availability being highly seasonal, during the 'lean season', solitary females feed just as well as individuals in groups of five females; but groups of two to four females suffer lower food intakes. The evolution of sociality would have required a strong advantage for ancestral lions to start living in pairs or trios, so group hunting is not the answer.\\n\\nNursery groups serve as a defensive formation. After hiding their cubs for the first few weeks of life, mothers bring them to the rest of the pride and nursing mothers become almost inseparable for the next two years. But the members of nursery groups suffer lower feeding success, so the behavior does not derive from any advantage of group foraging.\\n\\nOutside males are always looking for mating opportunities. If they encounter a mother with small cubs, they will kill the cubs to induce her to return to mating condition. Resident males patrol the edges of the pride territory, and outside males sometimes encounter the nursery group unaccompanied. Cubs in a nursery group are far more likely to survive these encounters than cubs raised by singleton mothers. Groups of mothers can successfully counter-attack the invading males, whereas a female has no chance one-on-one against a fully grown male. Thus, nursery group formation clearly increases female reproductive success. However, most other felids, such as leopards, tigers, etc., also show similar infanticide by incoming males. So, defense against infanticide doesn't explain why lions are the only social cats.","question":"The author mentions felids such as leopards, tigers etc., to indicate that","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"nursery group formation gives adaptive advantage in the reproduction of lions.","B":"these felids have the ability to attack successfully one-on-one against fully grown males, defending their cubs.","C":"sociality in lions cannot be fully explained by reproductive success in lions.","D":"their reproductive success is less than that of female lions forming a pride."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"sociality in lions cannot be fully explained by reproductive success in lions.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"contradicts_passage","B":"out_of_scope","D":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"The author first states that nursery group formation increases female reproductive success. Then he mentions that other felids with the same danger of infanticide do not show this sociality. This implies that sociality does not fully explain reproductive success — if it did, other felids facing the same threat would also be social. Choice A is incorrect because this is what the author tries to disprove by mentioning the felids. Choice B is incorrect because we cannot infer from the information given that the other felids can successfully protect their offspring one-on-one. Choice C is CORRECT as per the reasons given above. Choice D is incorrect because the comparison between reproductive successes cannot be inferred.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E3_019","exercise":"exercise_3","passage_id":"RC_E3_P4","passage":"Why do lions live in groups? The traditional explanation for lion sociality has been cooperative hunting, but recent research shows that they do not hunt as cooperatively as believed. In fact, lions only hunt cooperatively whenever required. If their best chance for meal is a large dangerous prey like a Cape buffalo, lions certainly do pull together. But if the prey is relatively easy for a singleton to capture, the rest of the pride is more likely to cheer on their hunting companion rather than join the hunt. Hence, we often see a lone female pursuing a warthog while the rest of the pride merely watches.\\n\\nConsequently, individual lions don't always feed better in larger groups. Food availability being highly seasonal, during the 'lean season', solitary females feed just as well as individuals in groups of five females; but groups of two to four females suffer lower food intakes. The evolution of sociality would have required a strong advantage for ancestral lions to start living in pairs or trios, so group hunting is not the answer.\\n\\nNursery groups serve as a defensive formation. After hiding their cubs for the first few weeks of life, mothers bring them to the rest of the pride and nursing mothers become almost inseparable for the next two years. But the members of nursery groups suffer lower feeding success, so the behavior does not derive from any advantage of group foraging.\\n\\nOutside males are always looking for mating opportunities. If they encounter a mother with small cubs, they will kill the cubs to induce her to return to mating condition. Resident males patrol the edges of the pride territory, and outside males sometimes encounter the nursery group unaccompanied. Cubs in a nursery group are far more likely to survive these encounters than cubs raised by singleton mothers. Groups of mothers can successfully counter-attack the invading males, whereas a female has no chance one-on-one against a fully grown male. Thus, nursery group formation clearly increases female reproductive success. However, most other felids, such as leopards, tigers, etc., also show similar infanticide by incoming males. So, defense against infanticide doesn't explain why lions are the only social cats.","question":"Which of the following can be expected of sociality to have a strong advantage for the ancestral lions in hunting?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Food availability was not as seasonal as it is today and food availability was almost evenly spread across the year.","B":"A group of five lions were less successful in hunting than a group of three or four, but a group of two was more successful than a single lion.","C":"Food intake was higher in a pair than it was in a single animal and food intake was higher in a trio than in a pair.","D":"A single lion was as successful in hunting as is a group of lions, and sometimes, the former is more successful than the latter."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Food intake was higher in a pair than it was in a single animal and food intake was higher in a trio than in a pair.","difficulty":"hard","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"out_of_scope","B":"contradicts_passage","D":"contradicts_passage"},"explanation":"For sociality to have a strong advantage in hunting, the larger the group, the higher the advantage in successful hunting should be the pattern. Choice A is incorrect because seasonality of food cannot be an advantage for sociality — it addresses food distribution, not group hunting advantage. Choice B is incorrect because this posits that a larger group is less successful — this is a disadvantage of sociality, not an advantage. Choice C is CORRECT — if food intake consistently increases as group size grows (pair > single, trio > pair), this shows a clear, scalable advantage to sociality in hunting. Choice D is incorrect because this argues against sociality — if a single lion is as successful as a group, there is no advantage to grouping.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E3_020","exercise":"exercise_3","passage_id":"RC_E3_P4","passage":"Why do lions live in groups? The traditional explanation for lion sociality has been cooperative hunting, but recent research shows that they do not hunt as cooperatively as believed. In fact, lions only hunt cooperatively whenever required. If their best chance for meal is a large dangerous prey like a Cape buffalo, lions certainly do pull together. But if the prey is relatively easy for a singleton to capture, the rest of the pride is more likely to cheer on their hunting companion rather than join the hunt. Hence, we often see a lone female pursuing a warthog while the rest of the pride merely watches.\\n\\nConsequently, individual lions don't always feed better in larger groups. Food availability being highly seasonal, during the 'lean season', solitary females feed just as well as individuals in groups of five females; but groups of two to four females suffer lower food intakes. The evolution of sociality would have required a strong advantage for ancestral lions to start living in pairs or trios, so group hunting is not the answer.\\n\\nNursery groups serve as a defensive formation. After hiding their cubs for the first few weeks of life, mothers bring them to the rest of the pride and nursing mothers become almost inseparable for the next two years. But the members of nursery groups suffer lower feeding success, so the behavior does not derive from any advantage of group foraging.\\n\\nOutside males are always looking for mating opportunities. If they encounter a mother with small cubs, they will kill the cubs to induce her to return to mating condition. Resident males patrol the edges of the pride territory, and outside males sometimes encounter the nursery group unaccompanied. Cubs in a nursery group are far more likely to survive these encounters than cubs raised by singleton mothers. Groups of mothers can successfully counter-attack the invading males, whereas a female has no chance one-on-one against a fully grown male. Thus, nursery group formation clearly increases female reproductive success. However, most other felids, such as leopards, tigers, etc., also show similar infanticide by incoming males. So, defense against infanticide doesn't explain why lions are the only social cats.","question":"The passage suggests all of the following EXCEPT:","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"In lions, females are more active in hunting than males.","B":"The size of the prey decides whether lions undertake cooperative hunting or solitary hunting.","C":"Lion cubs in nursery groups have less survival chance than those being brought up individually.","D":"Infanticide in lions can be prevented more effectively by a group of mothers than by a single mother."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Lion cubs in nursery groups have less survival chance than those being brought up individually.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"premise_restatement","B":"premise_restatement","D":"premise_restatement"},"explanation":"Choice A is a proper inference based on the second and third sentences and the last sentence of the first paragraph — lone females pursue prey while the rest of the pride watches, indicating females are active hunters. Choice B is a correct inference from the first paragraph — cooperative hunting is triggered by large dangerous prey, solitary hunting when prey is easy. Choice C is CORRECT — this contradicts the passage. The passage explicitly states that cubs in a nursery group are far more likely to survive encounters with invading males than cubs raised by singleton mothers, meaning nursery groups have greater survival chance, not less. Choice D is a proper inference from the last paragraph — groups of mothers can successfully counter-attack while a single female has no chance one-on-one.","time_benchmark_seconds":75}]`);export{e as default};
